Chemistry parameters
----------

monomer size = 2,3,4 
extender size = 3,4,5 
(no checks implemented, can be longer in principle. Middle chain types need to be double checked)

If you put both sizes to 0, the system falls back to a "default" simple system with 
dumbells for monomer and extender, and a crosslinker with 5 beads, 4 reactive ends bonded to one central particle. 
In this case there are no angles.  


Physical parameters
----------

I used: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0032386123004433 to decide on 
an angle with stiffness 5, which should lead to a T_m of ~ 1kT, and then we simulate at 0.9kT 
i.e below the melting temperature at a density of 0.9 (read off fig. 2). The NpT ensemble 
simulation should allow for adjustments of this density as the system evolves. 
We use a different angle bending potential (because of what is available in hoomd 
but it should not matter). 

Signac allows for these parameters to be varied. 

Simple system of dumbell-monomers will not crystallize. 

Performance notes 
-----------------

This code is not very efficient!! It slows down with the number of bonds and angles. And, to a 
lesser degree, number of particles. Keep number of bonds below 1e4 (i.e N_monomers<4000) but
smaller is better for performance. If the system is larger than this, some GPUs will run out of 
memory (T4 and A10) and crash.  (error message is something like this: "slurmstepd: error: Detected 
1 oom-kill event(s) in StepId=10606636.batch. Some of your processes may have been killed by the 
cgroup out-of-memory handler." You will also see files "core.XXXX" appear.)


